  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1011 PM MDT Sat Aug 29 2026\n\nWestern areas into E AZ will remain favored for storms through \nMonday as the monsoon high shifts east over the mid-MS River Valley. \nA few showers or storms may redevelop tonight out west with the \nmodest moisture plume overhead and storm motion to the north. Most \nareas, including El Paso and Las Cruces, stay dry for Sunday with \nslightly better rain chances across the north. PWs rise to 1.1-1.2\" \nW of the Divide on Sun as the moisture plume nudges eastward, \nresulting in scattered convection capable of producing heavy \nrainfall (up to 2\"), focusing in Hidalgo Co. \n\nThe moisture plume nudges a bit further east on Mon, increasing \nstorm chances slightly area-wide. The heavy rain threat will remain \nelevated out west on Mon due to higher PWs, again favoring Hidalgo \nCo. Isolated activity is forecast into the evening for much of the \nlowlands with drier conditions for eastern areas. \n\nGlobal models are in agreement with the upper high focusing over the \nCarolinas/TN Valley into midweek and stretching across the southern \nPlains, keeping much of the monsoonal moisture south of us. Low mtn \nstorm chances are forecast from Tue onward as the lowlands stay \nmainly dry. The high looks to centralize over the southern Plains \nlate in the week and reinvigorate the moisture plume a bit. Areas to \nthe north and west appear best set up for storm chances to end the \nperiod. The placement of the high should keep any tropical remnants \nfrom the Pacific from reaching the Desert SW for Labor Day weekend.\n\nTemps will be steady through the week with highs 5-10 degrees above \nnormal. Lowland highs of 95-100F are forecast each day with slight \ncooling on Tue. Winds remain light through midweek, shifting more W \nby Mon.",
